Oh dear... let's see... killing aggressive monsters will make your god happy.
Sacrificing the right, unspoiled, corpses will make him very happy.
Being nasty to your pet or peaceful monsters will make him angry. Killing your
pet, sacrificing THE wrong corpses (e.g. humans if you are not chaotic), 
desacreting his altar and such will make him very angry.
It isn't actually as simple as that, but this will give you some idea...


>   2) It seems like everybody is having some intense success at posting
>    	scores in the 100,000+ range.  Anyone willing to detail the main
>	beginner mistakes that would help me get over the 10,000 point hump?

Simply: stay alive.
If you can survive the mines and reach the quest, you should have your score
around  ~200k. If it is that you have troubles with staying alive, try playing
barbarians. They seldom have any major difficulties (until they meet their 
quest nemesis... my first barbarian quest ended disastrously: before I had 
a chance to hit Thoth Amon, I was surrounded by minotaurs. The next thing I
saw was the all too familiar "You die...")

Valkyries and samurais are good at staying alive, too, but if you play either
of these, you should try to get poison resistance _AS SOON AS POSSIBLE_!
This cannot be too strongly emphasized. I have lost an incredible amount
of samurais (no valkyries, though, dunno why) to "The poison was deadly..."
It's... annoying. Mildly put.


>   3) Who is your favorite character?

I like different classes for different reasons:
Barbarians are *THE* survivalist class in the game. Real butt-kickers in close
combat, poison resistance enables them to eat almost anything (no starving to
death) and protects against some quick deaths. Also, they are neutral, which
means they can have a nice selection of artifacts, use the PYEC abuse (though
I never do that) and become lawful OR chaotic - as necessary - with a helm
of opposite alignment.

Valkyries are also good at staying alive. They get Mjollnir as their first
weapon by sacrificing. The orb of fate has one nice power, too. And valkyries
are neutral as well.

Samurais begin with the intrinsic speed, which makes low levels easy, and can
fight as well as the two classes above. Their quest artifact is a truely awe-
some weapon (though I often use Grayswandir instead of it...)

Priest can immediately see if an item is cursed/blessed, which makes id'ing
things easy. (You can, for example, try to wear uncursed/blessed rings or 
amulets...)

Wizards can begin the game with *REALLY* powerful items, but they are wimps
in combat. Their cloak of magic resistance is a very nice item.

